### What is Kafka Streams?

Kafka Streams is a library, not a server, so you add it as a dependency and run it inside your Java application. You configure it with an application id, bootstrap servers, and default serdes (serializer-deserializer pairs). The application id names the consumer group and the internal topics.

```java
package com.bank.streams;

import org.apache.kafka.streams.StreamsConfig;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.Serdes;
import java.util.Properties;

public class StreamsConfigDemo {
    public static Properties createConfig() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(StreamsConfig.APPLICATION_ID_CONFIG, "transaction-normalizer");
        props.put(StreamsConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(StreamsConfig.DEFAULT_KEY_SERDE_CLASS_CONFIG, Serdes.String().getClass());
        props.put(StreamsConfig.DEFAULT_VALUE_SERDE_CLASS_CONFIG, Serdes.String().getClass());

        System.out.println("Kafka Streams config created");
        System.out.println("  Application ID: transaction-normalizer");
        System.out.println("  Default serdes: String/String");
        return props;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        createConfig();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Kafka Streams config created
  Application ID: transaction-normalizer
  Default serdes: String/String
```

### How do you build the topology?

You describe the processing as a topology using the StreamsBuilder DSL. `builder.stream("input-topic")` creates a KStream. The `mapValues` transformation changes only the value, and `.to("output-topic")` writes the result.

```java
package com.bank.streams;

import org.apache.kafka.streams.StreamsBuilder;
import org.apache.kafka.streams.kstream.KStream;
import org.apache.kafka.streams.Topology;

public class UppercaseTopology {
    public static Topology build() {
        StreamsBuilder builder = new StreamsBuilder();

        // Source: read from raw-transactions topic
        KStream<String, String> rawStream = builder.stream("raw-transactions");

        // Transform: uppercase the transaction description
        KStream<String, String> normalizedStream = rawStream
            .mapValues(value -> value.toUpperCase());

        // Sink: write to normalized-transactions topic
        normalizedStream.to("normalized-transactions");

        Topology topology = builder.build();
        System.out.println("Topology built: raw-transactions -> uppercase -> normalized-transactions");
        return topology;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        build();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Topology built: raw-transactions -> uppercase -> normalized-transactions
```

### How do you write the complete application?

The complete app creates the config, builds the topology, starts the KafkaStreams instance, and adds a shutdown hook.

```java
package com.bank.streams;

import org.apache.kafka.streams.KafkaStreams;
import org.apache.kafka.streams.StreamsBuilder;
import org.apache.kafka.streams.StreamsConfig;
import org.apache.kafka.streams.kstream.KStream;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.Serdes;
import java.util.Properties;
import java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch;

public class TransactionNormalizerApp {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Configuration
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(StreamsConfig.APPLICATION_ID_CONFIG, "transaction-normalizer");
        props.put(StreamsConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "kafka-1:9092");
        props.put(StreamsConfig.DEFAULT_KEY_SERDE_CLASS_CONFIG, Serdes.String().getClass());
        props.put(StreamsConfig.DEFAULT_VALUE_SERDE_CLASS_CONFIG, Serdes.String().getClass());

        // Topology
        StreamsBuilder builder = new StreamsBuilder();
        KStream<String, String> rawStream = builder.stream("raw-transactions");
        rawStream.mapValues(String::toUpperCase).to("normalized-transactions");

        // Start
        KafkaStreams streams = new KafkaStreams(builder.build(), props);
        CountDownLatch latch = new CountDownLatch(1);

        Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(new Thread(() -> {
            streams.close();
            latch.countDown();
            System.out.println("Streams app shut down cleanly");
        }));

        try {
            streams.start();
            System.out.println("Transaction normalizer started");
            System.out.println("  Input:  raw-transactions");
            System.out.println("  Output: normalized-transactions");
            latch.await();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.exit(1);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Transaction normalizer started
  Input:  raw-transactions
  Output: normalized-transactions
```

### Why use mapValues instead of map?

`mapValues` transforms only the value and leaves the key and partition unchanged. If you used `map` and changed the key, Streams would repartition the data, which is more expensive and can reorder records.

| Operation | Changes Key? | Repartition? | Preserves Order? |
|---|---|---|---|
| `mapValues` | No | No | Yes |
| `map` | Yes | Yes (if key changes) | May reorder |
| `flatMapValues` | No | No | Yes |
| `flatMap` | Yes | Yes (if key changes) | May reorder |

```java
package com.bank.streams;

public class MapVsMapValues {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // mapValues: only value changes, key stays, no repartition
        System.out.println("mapValues: key=SAV2001, value='debit 50000' -> 'DEBIT 50000'");
        System.out.println("  Same key, same partition, same order -- correct");

        // map: key changes, triggers repartition
        System.out.println("map: key=SAV2001 -> key=SAVINGS-SAV2001");
        System.out.println("  New key, repartition needed, possible reorder -- expensive");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
mapValues: key=SAV2001, value='debit 50000' -> 'DEBIT 50000'
  Same key, same partition, same order -- correct
map: key=SAV2001 -> key=SAVINGS-SAV2001
  New key, repartition needed, possible reorder -- expensive
```

### How does Kafka Streams scale and recover?

A Streams app scales by partition. The input topic's partitions are divided among running instances of the same application id, exactly like a consumer group. If an instance dies, its partitions are reassigned to survivors.

```text
  6-partition input topic, 3 instances:

  Instance 1: partitions 0, 1
  Instance 2: partitions 2, 3
  Instance 3: partitions 4, 5

  If Instance 2 dies:
  Instance 1: partitions 0, 1, 2
  Instance 3: partitions 3, 4, 5
```

### How do you test a Kafka Streams topology?

Use the `TopologyTestDriver` for unit testing without a running broker.

```java
package com.bank.streams;

import org.apache.kafka.streams.StreamsBuilder;
import org.apache.kafka.streams.TopologyTestDriver;
import org.apache.kafka.streams.TestInputTopic;
import org.apache.kafka.streams.TestOutputTopic;
import org.apache.kafka.streams.StreamsConfig;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.Serdes;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer;
import org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer;
import java.util.Properties;

public class TopologyTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Build topology
        StreamsBuilder builder = new StreamsBuilder();
        builder.<String, String>stream("raw-transactions")
            .mapValues(String::toUpperCase)
            .to("normalized-transactions");

        // Test config
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(StreamsConfig.APPLICATION_ID_CONFIG, "test");
        props.put(StreamsConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "dummy:1234");
        props.put(StreamsConfig.DEFAULT_KEY_SERDE_CLASS_CONFIG, Serdes.String().getClass());
        props.put(StreamsConfig.DEFAULT_VALUE_SERDE_CLASS_CONFIG, Serdes.String().getClass());

        try (TopologyTestDriver driver = new TopologyTestDriver(builder.build(), props)) {
            TestInputTopic<String, String> input = driver.createInputTopic(
                "raw-transactions", new StringSerializer(), new StringSerializer());
            TestOutputTopic<String, String> output = driver.createOutputTopic(
                "normalized-transactions", new StringDeserializer(), new StringDeserializer());

            input.pipeInput("SAV2001", "debit 50000 for Rajesh");
            String result = output.readValue();
            System.out.println("Input:  debit 50000 for Rajesh");
            System.out.println("Output: " + result);
            System.out.println("Test passed: " + "DEBIT 50000 FOR RAJESH".equals(result));
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Input:  debit 50000 for Rajesh
Output: DEBIT 50000 FOR RAJESH
Test passed: true
```

### What about exactly-once processing in Kafka Streams?

Setting the processing guarantee to `exactly_once_v2` makes the read-process-write atomic using Kafka transactions.

```java
package com.bank.streams;

import org.apache.kafka.streams.StreamsConfig;
import java.util.Properties;

public class ExactlyOnceConfig {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(StreamsConfig.PROCESSING_GUARANTEE_CONFIG, "exactly_once_v2");
        System.out.println("Processing guarantee: exactly_once_v2");
        System.out.println("  Read, transform, and write are atomic");
        System.out.println("  Uses Kafka transactions under the hood");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Processing guarantee: exactly_once_v2
  Read, transform, and write are atomic
  Uses Kafka transactions under the hood
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

For stateful operations like aggregations, Streams keeps local state stores backed by changelog topics. For our stateless uppercase app, failover is trivial because there is no state. Streams preserves per-key ordering because records for a key stay in one partition. The topology can be extracted and unit-tested with TopologyTestDriver without a running broker.
